{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '30,66p'; rg -n '\\\\\\\\(sub)*section|\\\\\\\\cite' paper.tex\"","aggregated_output":"    30\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n    31\t\\centering\n    32\t\\includegraphics[width=.98\\textwidth,height=1.6in,keepaspectratio]{figures/composition_reliability_overview.png}\n    33\t\\caption{Study overview. Training data determine centers and local scales, including internal rank-based selection. A separate calibration sample determines conformal thresholds. Evaluation distinguishes membership of observed compositions from mass under a uniform-simplex reference distribution; neither estimand establishes conditional or shifted validity.}\n    34\t\\label{fig:overview}\n    35\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py]]\n    36\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py]]\n    37\t\\end{figure}\n    38\t\n    39\t\\section{Related Work}\n    40\t\\paragraph{Calibration and adaptation.}\n    41\tThe conformal framework provides finite-sample predictive coverage under exchangeability \\citep{Vovk2005}; inductive constructions separate model fitting and calibration \\citep{Papadopoulos2002}. Regression studies compare the statistical and computational behavior of full and split conformal inference \\citep{Lei2018}, while jackknife+ uses leave-one-out predictions to account for fitting variability \\citep{Barber2021Jackknife}. Here the calibration procedure is ordinary split conformal: internal cross-fitting estimates scale information but is not jackknife+ calibration. Locally adaptive intervals have been developed through quantile regression \\citep{Romano2019,Sesia2020}, conditional density estimation \\citep{Lei2014,Izbicki2020}, and localized conformal constructions \\citep{Guan2023}. These motivate adaptation without establishing that our particular scalar correction is optimal. Selection and aggregation raise a separate issue from calibrating one fixed score \\citep{Yang2024}; our fitting choices are confined to training data.\n    42\t\n    43\t\\paragraph{What coverage does not imply.}\n    44\tConditional validity requires care even for inductive predictors \\citep{Vovk2012}. Distribution-free conditional predictive inference has fundamental limits \\citep{Barber2021Limits}, so pooled coverage should not be promoted to a feature-conditional guarantee. Under covariate shift, weighted conformal methods use covariate likelihood ratios \\citep{Tibshirani2019}; broader departures from exchangeability require their own analysis \\citep{Barber2023Beyond}. Adaptive conformal inference instead studies an online coverage-frequency target \\citep{Gibbs2021}. These extensions are not implemented here. Our shifted evaluation is a stress test of a fixed, unweighted calibration rule, not a comparison with shift-aware methods.\n    45\t\n    46\t\\paragraph{Composition and statistical measurement.}\n    47\tCompositional analysis treats ratios and the simplex as central objects \\citep{Aitchison1982,Pawlowsky2015}; logistic-normal distributions provide transformed-normal models on that space \\citep{Aitchison1980}. Isometric logratio (ILR) coordinates give an orthonormal representation \\citep{Egozcue2003}, but orthonormality in logratio geometry does not preserve uniform-simplex mass. Our centers use ridge regression \\citep{Hoerl1970}, and the scale correction is a shrunk kernel regression of residual moments in the spirit of \\citet{Nadaraya1964}. Uncertainty reporting uses resampling across independent seed blocks \\citep{Efron1979,Davison1997} and distinguishes those intervals from within-sample Wilson coverage intervals \\citep{Brown2001}. The contribution is the resulting controlled audit, not a claim to originate any of these ingredients.\n    48\t\n    49\t\\section{Prediction Regions and Evaluation Targets}\n    50\t\\label{sec:regions}\n    51\t\\subsection{Problem setup and calibration}\n    52\tLet $X\\in\\R^6$ and $Y\\in\\simplex=\\{y\\in(0,1)^K:\\sum_k y_k=1\\}$. We predict a region $C_\\alpha(X)$ for an independent \\emph{observed response} $Y$, at target coverage $1-\\alpha$. No class label or latent multinomial parameter is evaluated. A fixed Helmert matrix $B\\in\\R^{K\\times(K-1)}$ satisfies $B^\\top B=I$ and $B^\\top\\mathbf 1=0$; the representation is\n    53\t\\begin{equation}\n    54\t z(y)=B^\\top\\log y,\\qquad h(z)=\\operatorname{softmax}(Bz).\n    55\t\\end{equation}\n    56\tFitted center $\\widehat\\mu(x)\\in\\R^{K-1}$ and positive scales $\\widehat s_j(x)$ define the nonconformity score\n    57\t\\begin{equation}\n    58\t A(x,y)=\\max_{1\\leq j<K}\\frac{|z_j(y)-\\widehat\\mu_j(x)|}{\\widehat s_j(x)}.\n    59\t \\label{eq:score}\n    60\t\\end{equation}\n    61\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py#_basis]]\n    62\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#method_records]]\n    63\tGiven $m$ independent calibration scores, use the $r$th smallest score with $r=\\lceil(m+1)(1-\\alpha)\\rceil$, or $+\\infty$ if $r>m$. The region consists of positive compositions satisfying $A(x,y)\\leq q_\\alpha$. It is an axis-aligned box in the fixed ILR basis, mapped back to the simplex; it is not a full-covariance ellipsoid. The finite-rank rule, including its infinite-threshold case, is owned by the evaluation code rather than the fitted predictor. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#quantile]]\n    64\t\n    65\tConditional on the training data, exchangeable calibration and test scores give $\\Pr\\{Y\\in C_\\alpha(X)\\}\\geq1-\\alpha$ by the standard conformal rank argument \\citep{Lei2018}. Ties are handled conservatively by the non-strict membership inequality. This is a statement about a random calibration sample and future response, not a guarantee for every realized test set, every fixed feature vector, or every group. Training-only adaptation leaves this argument intact because the final calibration responses do not enter score selection. The argument does not apply unchanged to our shifted test sample.\n    66\t\n19:\\section{Introduction}\n22:Split conformal prediction supplies a natural starting point because its marginal coverage guarantee separates fitting a predictive score from calibrating its threshold \\citep{Papadopoulos2002,Lei2018,Angelopoulos2021}. Compositional geometry nevertheless leaves important evaluation choices. Logratio representations remove the sum constraint but change how region size is measured \\citep{Aitchison1982,Egozcue2003}. Heteroscedastic scaling can redistribute widths without altering the calibration rule. Consequently, judging a region construction requires more than a single coverage number or a single transformed-space volume.\n39:\\section{Related Work}\n41:The conformal framework provides finite-sample predictive coverage under exchangeability \\citep{Vovk2005}; inductive constructions separate model fitting and calibration \\citep{Papadopoulos2002}. Regression studies compare the statistical and computational behavior of full and split conformal inference \\citep{Lei2018}, while jackknife+ uses leave-one-out predictions to account for fitting variability \\citep{Barber2021Jackknife}. Here the calibration procedure is ordinary split conformal: internal cross-fitting estimates scale information but is not jackknife+ calibration. Locally adaptive intervals have been developed through quantile regression \\citep{Romano2019,Sesia2020}, conditional density estimation \\citep{Lei2014,Izbicki2020}, and localized conformal constructions \\citep{Guan2023}. These motivate adaptation without establishing that our particular scalar correction is optimal. Selection and aggregation raise a separate issue from calibrating one fixed score \\citep{Yang2024}; our fitting choices are confined to training data.\n44:Conditional validity requires care even for inductive predictors \\citep{Vovk2012}. Distribution-free conditional predictive inference has fundamental limits \\citep{Barber2021Limits}, so pooled coverage should not be promoted to a feature-conditional guarantee. Under covariate shift, weighted conformal methods use covariate likelihood ratios \\citep{Tibshirani2019}; broader departures from exchangeability require their own analysis \\citep{Barber2023Beyond}. Adaptive conformal inference instead studies an online coverage-frequency target \\citep{Gibbs2021}. These extensions are not implemented here. Our shifted evaluation is a stress test of a fixed, unweighted calibration rule, not a comparison with shift-aware methods.\n47:Compositional analysis treats ratios and the simplex as central objects \\citep{Aitchison1982,Pawlowsky2015}; logistic-normal distributions provide transformed-normal models on that space \\citep{Aitchison1980}. Isometric logratio (ILR) coordinates give an orthonormal representation \\citep{Egozcue2003}, but orthonormality in logratio geometry does not preserve uniform-simplex mass. Our centers use ridge regression \\citep{Hoerl1970}, and the scale correction is a shrunk kernel regression of residual moments in the spirit of \\citet{Nadaraya1964}. Uncertainty reporting uses resampling across independent seed blocks \\citep{Efron1979,Davison1997} and distinguishes those intervals from within-sample Wilson coverage intervals \\citep{Brown2001}. The contribution is the resulting controlled audit, not a claim to originate any of these ingredients.\n49:\\section{Prediction Regions and Evaluation Targets}\n51:\\subsection{Problem setup and calibration}\n65:Conditional on the training data, exchangeable calibration and test scores give $\\Pr\\{Y\\in C_\\alpha(X)\\}\\geq1-\\alpha$ by the standard conformal rank argument \\citep{Lei2018}. Ties are handled conservatively by the non-strict membership inequality. This is a statement about a random calibration sample and future response, not a guarantee for every realized test set, every fixed feature vector, or every group. Training-only adaptation leaves this argument intact because the final calibration responses do not enter score selection. The argument does not apply unchanged to our shifted test sample.\n67:\\subsection{Reliability, mass, and utility}\n84:\\subsection{Compared region constructions}\n89:\\section{Method: Training-Only Scale Adaptation}\n91:\\subsection{Rationale and base fits}\n96:\\subsection{Local residual moments and bounded shrinkage}\n110:\\subsection{Nested rank-based selection}\n125:\\section{Experimental Design and Uncertainty}\n127:\\subsection{Synthetic conditions and held-out protocol}\n144:\\subsection{Paired measurements and uncertainty}\n151:\\section{Reliability--Efficiency Results}\n153:\\subsection{Near-target IID behavior does not survive the shift}\n167:\\subsection{Aggregate utility and simplex mass favor different readings}\n182:\\subsection{Condition-specific comparisons rule out uniform dominance}\n199:\\subsection{Controls and what they identify}\n203:\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n219:\\section{Conclusion}\n229:\\section{Supplementary Information}\n232:\\subsection{Detailed selection specification and algebraic checks}\n244:\\subsection{Generator, target, and reproducibility specification}\n253:\\subsection{Complete condition-level results and paired comparisons}\n370:\\subsection{Feature-group reliability and information controls}\n383:\\subsection{Reference-mass resolution and degenerate controls}\n388:\\subsection{Evidence provenance and comparison scope}\n395:\\subsection{LLM Usage}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
