{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '89,150p' paper.tex; sed -n '203,239p' paper.tex\"","aggregated_output":"\\section{Method: Training-Only Scale Adaptation}\n\\label{sec:method}\n\\subsection{Rationale and base fits}\nThe design hypothesis is that feature-dependent residual variability makes constant-width ILR regions inefficient, and that a bounded, shrunk correction may allocate widths more usefully. The counter-hypothesis is equally important: a correction selected using a transformed-space size objective may not reduce simplex mass, especially under extrapolation. The study evaluates these possibilities through paired region comparisons, not through a claim of optimal scale estimation.\n\nThe design vector is $\\phi(x)=(1,x,x^{\\odot2},\\sin x,x_0x_1)$, with coordinatewise squares and sine terms. Ridge regression of $z(Y)$ on $\\phi(X)$ uses penalty 1. A second ridge fit, with penalty 10, predicts $\\log\\max(|z_j(Y)-\\widehat\\mu_j(X)|,0.02)$ from the same design. These base-scale residuals are in-sample; cross-fitting is used for the subsequent moment correction, not retroactively for the base log-residual fit. The intercept penalty is $10^{-8}$. Predicted centers are clipped to $[-100,100]$ and base scales to $[10^{-4},100]$. These are numerical bounds, not distributional assumptions. % @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py#_features]]\n\n\\subsection{Local residual moments and bounded shrinkage}\nDeterministic three-fold cross-fitting within training data yields held-out centers $\\widehat\\mu^{(-i)}$ and base scales $\\widehat s^{0,(-i)}$. For each training row define\n\\begin{equation}\n v_i=\\frac{1}{K-1}\\sum_{j=1}^{K-1}\n \\left(\\frac{z_j(Y_i)-\\widehat\\mu_j^{(-i)}(X_i)}{\\widehat s_j^{0,(-i)}(X_i)}\\right)^2.\n\\end{equation}\nTraining-only feature means and standard deviations standardize the covariates. With standardized distance $d_i(x)$, Gaussian weights $w_i(x)=\\exp\\{-d_i(x)^2/(2b^2)\\}$ yield\n\\begin{equation}\n R(x)=\\frac{\\sum_iw_i(x)v_i+\\lambda}{\\sum_iw_i(x)+\\lambda},\\qquad\n a(x)=\\exp\\!\\left\\{\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\log\\clip(R(x),1/4,4)\\right\\}.\n \\label{eq:correction}\n\\end{equation}\nThe final scales are $\\widehat s_j(x)=a(x)\\widehat s^0_j(x)$, with a common row-scalar further bounded to respect the allowed scale range. Shrinkage returns $R(x)$ toward one when kernel support is weak; $\\beta=0$ is the neutral correction. The scalar leaves relative ILR-coordinate scales unchanged at a given $x$. It does not learn new directions or correlations. % @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py#build_oof_residual_table]]\n\n\\subsection{Nested rank-based selection}\nThe finite family uses $\\beta\\in\\{0,0.5,1\\}$, $b\\in\\{0.5\\sqrt6,\\sqrt6\\}$, and $\\lambda\\in\\{10,50\\}$. For each of three outer training folds, the remaining rows fit both the base model and an inner cross-fitted moment table. The held-out fold is split into two halves, used in both orientations as pseudo-calibration and evaluation data. Each pseudo-calibration half gives a finite-rank radius $q_{f,\\alpha}$ at the same three miscoverage levels used for reporting. The selector minimizes the evaluation-row-weighted, level-averaged objective\n\\begin{equation}\n L(\\theta)=\\sum_f\\frac{|E_f|}{n_{\\rm train}}\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal A|}\n \\sum_{\\alpha\\in\\mathcal A}\\log\\left[\n \\frac1{|E_f|}\\sum_{i\\in E_f}(2q_{f,\\alpha})^{K-1}\n \\prod_{j=1}^{K-1}\\widehat s_{f,j,\\theta}(X_i)\\right],\n \\label{eq:selection}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $f$ indexes both orientations and $\\mathcal A=\\{0.05,0.10,0.20\\}$. The logarithm is applied to arithmetic mean box volume, evaluated stably, not to each row before averaging. Ties favor smaller $\\beta$. The selected correction is refitted using a full training moment table. % @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py#_select_correction]]\n\nCrucially, Eq.~\\eqref{eq:selection} measures \\emph{ILR-coordinate box volume}. The nonlinear inverse map does not have a constant simplex-volume Jacobian, so this is not Eq.~\\eqref{eq:mass}. A uniform multiplicative rescaling of all scales cancels against its conformal radius in the absence of clipping; improvement must come from changing allocation across inputs or coordinates, not merely multiplying all widths by a constant. The supplement derives this algebraic check. Neither property establishes that the selected correction improves the final mass or coverage.\n\nUnavailable finite ranks, nonpositive radii, or nonfinite objectives trigger a training-only Gaussian-loss selector over the same scale family; very small samples use a neutral correction. These are source-defined fallbacks. The retained results do not record how often each fallback was used or which setting was selected in each fit. We do not infer those frequencies from aggregate performance. Independent final calibration remains unchanged in every branch. % @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py#_select_correction_gaussian]]\n\n\\section{Experimental Design and Uncertainty}\n\\label{sec:design}\n\\subsection{Synthetic conditions and held-out protocol}\nThe available evidence covers one continuous-composition task, with six conditions: logistic-normal IID, Dirichlet IID, and shifted Dirichlet, each at $K\\in\\{3,8\\}$. Every condition uses six covariates, 1,024 training observations, 512 calibration observations, and 2,048 test observations. The held-out seeds are 1009, 1013, 1019, 1021, 1031, 1033, 1039, and 1049, with $\\alpha\\in\\{0.05,0.10,0.20\\}$. Each seed is a joint repeated unit containing all conditions and nominal levels, not eighteen independent replications. All recorded condition evaluations are complete. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/data_manifest.json]]\n\nFor IID samples, $X$ is uniform on $[-1.5,1.5]^6$. Let $W$ be the fixed sinusoidal coefficient matrix specified in the supplement, and define\n\\begin{equation}\n \\eta_j(x)=0.7\\sin((x^\\top W)_j)+0.3x_0\\cos j,\n \\qquad j=0,\\ldots,K-2.\n\\end{equation}\nLogistic-normal responses are $h(\\eta(X)+\\epsilon)$ with independent Gaussian coordinates of standard deviation 0.25. The Dirichlet construction uses parameter vector $\\tau(x)h(\\eta(x))$, with $\\tau(x)=4+35/(1+\\exp(2x_0))$. The generator draws gamma coordinates, floors them at $10^{-12}$, and closes the vector to sum to one. The evaluated law is this numerically floored distribution, not an unfloored boundary model. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#generate_arrays]]\n\nShifted conditions change only the test covariate law: coordinates are drawn from $N(0.8,1)$ and clipped to $[-2.5,3]$. Training and calibration remain IID uniform, and the conditional response recipe is unchanged. This changes both feature frequencies and support. In particular, part of the test distribution lies outside training support; ordinary likelihood-ratio weighting cannot by itself recover information there. The comparison therefore probes a demanding shift, not an overlap-controlled weighting scenario. Its mechanisms are not separately manipulated.\n\nThe evaluated scale-adaptive configuration is frozen for the held-out analysis. Development configuration scores are not pooled with held-out measurements, and we do not compare an original initializer without matched final evidence. The evaluator reports completed qualification and passing information/state controls. This establishes completion of its specified checks, not population superiority or shift validity. Missing selector-specific ablations remain missing evidence rather than measured failures. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/status]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/control_check_pass_rate]]\n\n\\subsection{Paired measurements and uncertainty}\nEvery method sees the same train/calibration/test data within a seed and condition. Mass uses 128 randomly selected test contexts and a shared set of 1,024 uniform-simplex probes. Pairing the contexts and probes reduces avoidable comparison noise, although it does not remove Monte Carlo error. One hit per context changes its mass estimate by $1/1024$. A zero-hit context has the reported one-sided 95\\% binomial upper bound $1-0.05^{1/1024}=0.00292$; zero hits do not establish zero region mass. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases/0/methods/scaled_ilr/0]]\n\nThe retained evaluator supplies paired percentile bootstrap intervals for method differences, resampling the eight seeds with 20,000 draws. Its identical cross-seed uncertainty object is repeated inside seed wrappers; we deduplicate it rather than treating these copies as new data. Utility comparisons additionally include endpoints using tail probability $0.05/(2\\cdot54)$ for the 54 case-by-level-by-baseline contrasts. We retain these adjusted endpoints in the supplement, but finite-bootstrap extreme quantiles with few seed blocks are descriptive and not exact familywise certificates. Mass intervals have no corresponding multiplicity adjustment. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#uncertainty_by_case]]\n\nFor the coverage curves, overall utility intervals, and feature-group mean coverage, we derive additional descriptive percentile intervals by jointly resampling seed blocks, preserving all condition and level records within a draw. The saved analysis uses 20,000 draws and bootstrap seed 20261117. These are analyses of retained measurements, not additional experiments. Per-seed Wilson intervals characterize test-membership sampling for one fitted/calibrated instance; we neither average their endpoints nor substitute them for cross-seed uncertainty. No confidence interval here captures arbitrary generator changes or adaptation to the held-out findings. % @evidence[[analysis/summary.json]]\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\paragraph{What the positive comparisons establish.}\nAt the evaluated center and data budget, the scaling package can improve utility and reduce simplex mass relative to removing scaling. It also provides large paired gains against simplex max in the higher-dimensional IID Dirichlet condition. These are valid configuration-level comparisons, with matched data and reported seed variation. They are not evidence that every constituent is needed, nor that the rank-based objective is preferable to Gaussian-loss selection. The final measurements contain no matched selector-only comparison. Development configurations change multiple choices and use a different evidence split, so their scores cannot fill that gap.\n\n\\paragraph{Why the negative comparisons matter.}\nThe mismatch between the selection objective and the evaluation reference measure is an explicit limitation of the construction. The result is compatible with that mismatch, but does not isolate it as the cause of unfavorable mass differences. The shift also jointly changes support, feature frequencies, and the frequency of high-variability responses. Its adverse coverage cannot be attributed solely to extrapolation, scale misspecification, or one design choice without additional controlled evidence. Standard exchangeability theory explains why no unchanged guarantee is available, not the magnitude of the observed deficit.\n\n\\paragraph{Uncertainty and selection scope.}\nOnly the retained seed blocks quantify repetition. The audit framing is retrospective; its intervals do not adjust for choosing the manuscript's emphasis. Paired resampling respects dependence across conditions and nominal levels, but few independent blocks limit precision, especially at multiplicity-adjusted tails. Conditional-on-fit Wilson intervals, Monte Carlo mass diagnostics, and seed-bootstrap intervals answer different questions. Shared probes introduce dependence across contexts, so an average of per-context standard errors is not a confidence interval for the overall mass. The main comparisons and the overall utility gap are descriptive; no claim is made that they remain significant after arbitrary selection of endpoints or across unobserved tasks.\n\n\\paragraph{Domain and geometric scope.}\nThe study has no public-data experiment, alternative ILR basis, learned covariance region, structural-zero response, or independent boundary-face reference measure. Positivity flooring makes logratios computable but is not a principled solution for true zeros. Uniform-simplex mass is a clearly defined reference measure, not a universal notion of user-relevant uncertainty. Its dimension dependence and finite probe resolution can hide small or boundary-concentrated regions. Conversely, a smaller mass need not imply better prediction for the actual response law. These limitations prevent conclusions about classification label sets, latent probability regions, or general deployment reliability.\n\n\\paragraph{Practical interpretation.}\nThe evidence recommends an evaluation discipline rather than a universal method choice: specify the response being covered, state the reference measure, compare matched configurations, retain condition-specific failures, and distinguish marginal from conditional and shifted validity. Aggregate utility can be useful for summarization only after its components are visible. Where support changes or calibration is reused for selection, assumptions must be reassessed rather than inferred from an apparently favorable score. Neither issue is repaired by a publication decision or by satisfying a completion check.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nScale-adaptive conformal regions for continuous compositions exhibit a measurable but bounded reliability--efficiency trade-off. In the evaluated synthetic settings, independent split calibration gives near-target IID behavior, and the complete scaling construction improves aggregate utility relative to the unscaled matched-center alternative. Against the simplex-coordinate maximum-residual baseline, however, the positive aggregate utility difference coexists with larger overall simplex mass, favorable higher-dimensional IID Dirichlet comparisons, and adverse shifted comparisons. No uniformly superior region construction emerges from these measurements.\n\nThe distinction between training-time ILR-box volume and evaluation-time uniform-simplex mass is therefore essential to interpreting the method. Equally essential is the distinction between IID calibration and shifted reliability: training-only adaptation preserves the standard calibration argument but does not make it shift-invariant. Further evidence would be needed to isolate the selector's contribution, assess support-preserving shifts, or study structural zeros and real responses. Within the available evidence, the defensible conclusion is that coverage, geometric efficiency, and assumptions must be audited separately, with configuration-level gains stated at their measured scope.\n\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{iclr2026_conference}\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\\label{sec:supplement}\n\n\\subsection{Detailed selection specification and algebraic checks}\nThe archived implementation is \\texttt{source\\_evidence/selected\\_model.py}; the evaluated entrypoint declaration is retained in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/selected\\_spec.toml}. Basis and design construction appear at lines 89--107; base fitting and moment tables at lines 114--152; smoothing and bounded scale correction at lines 154--185; Gaussian-loss fallback at lines 188--234; and rank-based selection at lines 237--352. These readable implementations, rather than a hash or a previous assessment, support the concrete method description. % @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py]]\n\nEach call to the deterministic fold constructor permutes the relevant training indices with NumPy random seed 0 and splits them into three parts. Feature standard deviations in moment tables are bounded below by $10^{-8}$, standardized residual scales by $10^{-4}$, and kernel queries are processed in batches of 128. Neutral settings share predictions even when their redundant bandwidth or prior parameters differ. The method caches moment-ratio evaluations for repeated bandwidth/prior pairs. The selector's outer pseudo-calibration halves and evaluation halves are disjoint from one another and from the rows fitting their models. Internal folds overlap across fits and must not be counted as independent scientific replications. % @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py#_training_folds]]\n\nTo check global-scale invariance, replace $s(x)$ by $c\\,s(x)$ for constant $c>0$, ignoring numerical clipping. Every calibration score is divided by $c$, so its order-statistic radius becomes $q_\\alpha/c$. The resulting coordinate half-width is $(q_\\alpha/c)(c\\,s_j(x))=q_\\alpha s_j(x)$. Its ILR-box volume is unchanged:\n\\begin{equation}\n (2q_\\alpha/c)^{K-1}\\prod_{j=1}^{K-1}c\\,s_j(x)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
