{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '370,398p' paper.tex; cat global_memory_summary.md\"","aggregated_output":"\\subsection{Feature-group reliability and information controls}\nThe two observable audit groups are $x_0>0$ and $x_0\\leq0$. They are defined by an input, not by a realized response, and do not create group-specific calibration thresholds. Figure~\\ref{fig:groups} reports mean group coverage at nominal 0.90, with joint seed-block intervals. Each seed contributes its group fraction, rather than pooling all test rows as independent repeated fits. Group sample counts and per-seed Wilson intervals are retained in the final evidence. The visual comparison is descriptive and not a test of full conditional validity. % @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/feature_groups_alpha_010]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[ht]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=.98\\textwidth]{figures/feature_group_reliability.pdf}\n\\caption{Feature-group coverage of scaled ILR at nominal 0.90. Whiskers are descriptive seed-block 95\\% bootstrap intervals; the dashed line marks the target, not a groupwise guarantee. Both dimensions retain the shifted reliability deficit. These audit groups are not used for Mondrian calibration.}\n\\label{fig:groups}\n% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/feature_groups_alpha_010]]\n\\end{figure}\n\nThe constant-input control replaces each query feature vector by the training-feature mean before prediction. The permuted-input control rotates calibration rows and test rows separately, disrupting feature-response alignment without transferring labels. The constant-center reference uses the training mean in ILR coordinates and unit scales. These controls change information available at prediction time or the center itself; they are not interchangeable with the matched-center unscaled comparison. The evaluator supplies feature-control paired utility intervals in the retained uncertainty object. We do not use passing controls as evidence that every scientific validity threat has been excluded. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#evaluate_episode]]\n\n\\subsection{Reference-mass resolution and degenerate controls}\nUniform-simplex probes are shared across test contexts and methods within each condition/seed. The per-context reported standard-error diagnostic is $\\sqrt{\\widehat p(1-\\widehat p)/1024}$; the evaluator averages this diagnostic across contexts. It is not a valid replacement for a joint uncertainty calculation because the same probes are reused. At zero hits, the plugin diagnostic vanishes even though the one-sided binomial upper bound is positive. Reporting a zero mass estimate must therefore not be converted into a claim of zero geometric volume. This distinction is especially relevant for small high-dimensional regions. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#method_records]]\n\nThe whole-simplex construction has unit reference mass and covers every observed composition; its utility is zero because the efficiency factor vanishes. The zero-radius reference tests equality with a fitted point, not recovery of the original observation. These endpoints check that metric behavior is interpretable, but do not supply an efficiency comparison among useful calibrated sets. No separate integration over simplex faces or boundary strata is present. All measured mass refers to the full-dimensional uniform-simplex reference distribution. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#method_records]]\n\n\\subsection{Evidence provenance and comparison scope}\nThe independent final record reports completion, qualification, and successful specified checks. Qualification facts are grounded in the final qualification fields, independently of any development summary; they do not strengthen the statistical claims. The manuscript retains the shifted deficits and unfavorable region-size comparisons despite that outcome. The original initializer has no matched held-out result in this bundle, so no final comparison with it is reported. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/status]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/correct]]\n\nDevelopment evidence also contains a joint center/scale configuration and a Gaussian-selected local-moment configuration, archived in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/development\\_joint.py} (line 152) and \\texttt{source\\_evidence/development\\_gaussian.py} (line 185). They are provenance context, not final selector ablations: their scores are development measurements, not held-out measurements of the same controlled intervention. The main text consequently reports neither development-score margins nor a selector-specific causal advantage. Proposed alternative geometries, groupwise calibration, and latent-target diagnostics are not treated as completed measurements. Missing experiments remain limitations, not negative outcomes. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs]]\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\nLanguage models assisted hypothesis formulation, training-only method design, implementation preparation, reference-query planning, synthesis of retained measurements, manuscript drafting, and construction of plotting and validation scripts. The manuscript-writing assistant inspected archived implementations and final records, generated figures from existing measurements, and retrieved exact foundational references for the cited background. It did not generate new scientific evaluation episodes. The overview figure is prepared through an automated concept-figure workflow; quantitative plots are deterministic visualizations of retained records. No model-generated number is used as an empirical result. No human-supervision procedure or independent replication is asserted beyond the documented checks. The process logs are retained for audit without identifying authors.\n\n\\end{document}\n# Global Memory Summary\n\nUse as qualitative context only; do not use as quantitative grounding source.\n\n## Recent Entries\n\n- 2026-11-17 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Selected paired boundary-stratified conformal reliability and efficiency audit. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=No evaluated discovery is available to distill.\n  - evidence: Task permits generated compositional responses and covariates; histories are empty; incumbent manifest reports correct=true and combined_score=0.6417212587320733. Manifest-named source entrypoint was unavailable, so no implementation defect is inferred. Alternatives were broad label-only classification, log-ratio-only evaluation, and a larger latent-deconvolution project. No experiment or literature search was run.\n- 2026-11-17 | step=final | scope=idea\n  - summary: Wrote matching idea and rubric bundles; final status=idea_bundle_written, improvement unassessed. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=Structural artifact completion supplies no evaluated scientific conclusion.\n  - evidence: Hypothesis: marginal coverage and full-dimensional volume can conceal feature-group and boundary-reference weaknesses. Four implementation rubrics use concrete executable source snippets and one metric rubric defers to the canonical harness gate. One canonical idea-local memory target was used, with no alternate directory created.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-17T12:40:33.169960+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=0.6283805225369655, correct=True; scientific acceptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"baseline_utility_gap\": 0.009982014298422937, \"combined_score\": 0.6283805225369655, \"control_check_pass_rate\": 1.0, \"correct\": true, \"iid_coverage_error\": 0.017371961805555548, \"mean_volume\": 0.34381343700267647, \"shift_coverage_error\": 0.09747178819444445}\n- 2026-11-17 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Selected scale-only smooth local-moment correction with shrinkage to incumbent and neutral control. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=A single confounded lower aggregate score does not establish a mechanism-level scientific recommendation; this alternative remains untested.\n  - evidence: Prior joint center/scale candidate combined_score=0.6283805225369655 versus incumbent 0.6417212587320733, correct=true and control_check_pass_rate=1.0. Prior preparation documents training-only ILR center/scale controls and evaluator-owned calibration. Manifest-named incumbent source unavailable here. Rejected unsupported Mondrian/geometry changes, another joint rewrite, and constant-only scaling; no task program or literature search run.\n- 2026-11-17 | step=final | scope=idea\n  - summary: Wrote idea and matching rubric bundles; status=idea_bundle_written, improvement unassessed. Hypothesis isolates shrink-to-incumbent local scale correction with centers and calibration fixed. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=Static bundle completion supplies no new scientific performance or mechanism evidence.\n  - evidence: Both required JSON files contain matching rubric arrays. Three implementation verifications specify 4, 5, and 7 executable snippets. Write-set review confirms the sole local memory target ideas/02_training_only_shrunk_local_moment_scale_correction/memory.md. No computational validation or task-program execution performed.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-17T12:48:00.979568+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=0.6421289430111841, correct=True; scientific acceptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"baseline_utility_gap\": 0.013867224241875808, \"combined_score\": 0.6421289430111841, \"control_check_pass_rate\": 1.0, \"correct\": true, \"iid_coverage_error\": 0.018196614583333326, \"mean_volume\": 0.3331370883517795, \"shift_coverage_error\": 0.07864583333333335}\n- 2026-11-17 | step=final | scope=idea\n  - summary: Wrote Training-Only Conformal-Rank Scale Selection and matching rubric bundles; status=idea_bundle_written, new method unexecuted. Isolate the selector while preserving accepted centers and scale family. gate=updated; trigger=b; reason=The recorded accepted scale-only successor supplies the first evidence-backed promising method category, while its tiny unreplicated aggregate gain supports neither causal attribution nor a shift-coverage claim.\n  - evidence: Historical cycle 02 passed: combined_score=0.6421289430111841, correct=true, control_check_pass_rate=1.0, iid_coverage_error=0.018196614583333326, shift_coverage_error=0.07864583333333335, mean_volume=0.3331370883517795; prior incumbent recorded as 0.6417212587320733. Named results-relative incumbent source exposes training-only X/Y and Gaussian-loss scale selection; no evaluator inspection. Rejected tail-model/coordinate-shape changes for attribution and Mondrian controls as unsupported. Required bundles statically reviewed with matching rubric content; 4/5/7/5 literal implementation snippets. Sole cycle-local memory target is ideas/03_training_only_conformal_rank_scale_selection/memory.md. No new scientific computation or computational validation.\n- 2026-11-17T12:53:43.300362+00:00 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: generated idea bundle (auto-healed by idea.sh)\n  - evidence: idea_bundle.json + target_rubrics.json\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-17T12:57:27.030079+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=0.6450635814905741, correct=True; scientific acceptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"baseline_utility_gap\": 0.016801862721265826, \"combined_score\": 0.6450635814905741, \"control_check_pass_rate\": 1.0, \"correct\": true, \"iid_coverage_error\": 0.018120659722222213, \"mean_volume\": 0.3222492359302662, \"shift_coverage_error\": 0.11304253472222224}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json,re\nfrom pathlib import Path\ntex=Path('paper.tex').read_text()\nbib=Path('references.bib').read_text()\nprovenance=json.loads(Path('references_provenance.json').read_text())\nkeys=set(re.findall(r'@\\\\w+\\\\s*\\\\{\\\\s*([\"'^,]+)'\"',bib))\ncited={key.strip() for group in re.findall(r'\\\\\\\\cite\\\\w*\\\\{([\"'^}]+)'\"\\\\}',tex) for key in group.split(',')}\nprovenance_keys={reference['citation_key'] for reference in provenance['references']}\nprint('Bibliography entries:',len(keys),'distinct cited keys:',len(cited),'provenance references:',len(provenance_keys))\nprint('Missing bibliography keys:',sorted(cited-keys))\nprint('Missing provenance keys:',sorted(cited-provenance_keys))\nprint('Uncited bibliography keys:',sorted(keys-cited))\nprint('\\\\nMETHOD-SCOPE GROUNDING')\nfor claim in json.loads(Path('claims_grounding.json').read_text())['claims'][:22]:\n    print(claim['claim'][:500], 'sources=',claim.get('source_ids'))\nprint('\\\\nEXISTING ADAPTATION ABSTRACTS')\nfor key in ['Guan2023','Yang2024']:\n    entry=json.loads((Path('literature')/(key+'.json')).read_text())\n    print(key,entry.get('abstract',entry.get('message',{}).get('abstract','No abstract field')))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json,re\nfrom pathlib import Path\ntex=Path('paper.tex').read_text()\nbib=Path('references.bib').read_text()\nprovenance=json.loads(Path('references_provenance.json').read_text())\nkeys=set(re.findall(r'@\\\\w+\\\\s*\\\\{\\\\s*([\"'^,]+)'\"',bib))\ncited={key.strip() for group in re.findall(r'\\\\\\\\cite\\\\w*\\\\{([\"'^}]+)'\"\\\\}',tex) for key in group.split(',')}\nprovenance_keys={reference['citation_key'] for reference in provenance['references']}\nprint('Bibliography entries:',len(keys),'distinct cited keys:',len(cited),'provenance references:',len(provenance_keys))\nprint('Missing bibliography keys:',sorted(cited-keys))\nprint('Missing provenance keys:',sorted(cited-provenance_keys))\nprint('Uncited bibliography keys:',sorted(keys-cited))\nprint('\\\\nMETHOD-SCOPE GROUNDING')\nfor claim in json.loads(Path('claims_grounding.json').read_text())['claims'][:22]:\n    print(claim['claim'][:500], 'sources=',claim.get('source_ids'))\nprint('\\\\nEXISTING ADAPTATION ABSTRACTS')\nfor key in ['Guan2023','Yang2024']:\n    entry=json.loads((Path('literature')/(key+'.json')).read_text())\n    print(key,entry.get('abstract',entry.get('message',{}).get('abstract','No abstract field')))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"Bibliography entries: 25 distinct cited keys: 25 provenance references: 25\nMissing bibliography keys: []\nMissing provenance keys: []\nUncited bibliography keys: []\n\nMETHOD-SCOPE GROUNDING\nThe independent final evaluation completed, was qualified, and reports successful specified checks; qualification is not a superiority or shifted-validity result. sources= ['final.qualification.status', 'final.qualification.outcome', 'final.qualification.correct', 'final.metric.control_check_pass_rate']\nThe final study comprises eight seeds, six conditions (logistic-normal IID, Dirichlet IID, shifted Dirichlet at K=3 and 8), and alpha=0.05,0.10,0.20. All recorded cases are complete. sources= ['final.evidence.episodes']\nFinal seeds are 1009,1013,1019,1021,1031,1033,1039,1049; train/calibration/test sizes are 1024/512/2048 with six covariates. sources= ['final.evidence.episodes']\nPositive observed continuous compositions, not categorical labels or latent probabilities, are the response. The fixed Helmert ILR basis has K-1 coordinates. sources= ['final.evidence.episodes', 'result.top_program_01']\nThe design uses intercept, raw features, squares, sines and x0*x1. Center and log-residual ridge penalties are 1 and 10; intercept penalty is 1e-8; residual floor is .02. sources= ['final.evidence.episodes', 'result.top_program_01']\nCenters are clipped to [-100,100] and positive base and corrected scales to [1e-4,100]. The base log-residual fit uses in-sample residuals; correction moments are cross-fitted. sources= ['final.evidence.episodes', 'result.top_program_01']\nThree-fold training permutations use random seed 0. Moment features use standard deviation floor 1e-8; standardized residuals use scale floor 1e-4; queries are batched in 128 rows. sources= ['final.evidence.episodes', 'result.top_program_01']\nLocal moments average squared standardized ILR residuals over K-1 coordinates, Gaussian-smooth them over standardized covariates, and shrink the ratio toward 1 by prior mass. sources= ['final.evidence.episodes', 'result.top_program_01']\nMoment ratios are clipped to [.25,4]; a common row-scalar multiplies all ILR scales, preserving their within-row ratios. The correction blends are 0,.5,1; bandwidths .5*sqrt(6),sqrt(6); prior masses 10,50. sources= ['final.evidence.episodes', 'result.top_program_01']\nNested three-fold training selection uses swapped pseudo-calibration/evaluation halves; the row-weighted alpha-averaged log arithmetic mean ILR-box volume is minimized, with smaller blend breaking ties. sources= ['final.evidence.episodes', 'result.top_program_01']\nThe selector uses exact finite ranks, Gaussian-loss fallback for unavailable ranks or invalid radii/objectives, and a neutral tiny-sample fallback. Realized fallback frequencies and selected settings are not retained. sources= ['final.evidence.episodes', 'result.top_program_01']\nGlobal constant scale multiplication cancels its conformal radius, apart from clipping. At alpha .05, 18 internal calibration observations are insufficient for a finite rank; 19 suffice. Numerical invariance assertions are source checks, not a scientific ablation. sources= ['final.evidence.episodes', 'result.top_program_01']\nIndependent final calibration uses ceil((m+1)*(1-alpha)) order statistic with infinity when unavailable, and closed membership. It is not selected on calibration labels. sources= ['final.evidence.episodes', 'result.top_program_01']\nThe unscaled ILR comparator sets scales to one at the same center. Simplex max uses a joint coordinate maximum. Bonferroni calibrates K simplex coordinates at alpha/K, not ILR coordinates. sources= ['final.evidence.episodes', 'result.top_program_01']\nMass is uniform-simplex probability content estimated at 128 test contexts using 1024 shared Dirichlet(1) probes; utility equals (1-mass)*max(0,1-absolute_coverage_error/(1-alpha)). Errors are absolute before averaging. sources= ['final.evidence.episodes', 'result.top_program_01']\nIID covariates are uniform on [-1.5,1.5]^6, logistic-normal noise standard deviation is .25, Dirichlet concentration is 4+35/(1+exp(2*x0)), and gamma coordinates are floored at 1e-12 before closure. sources= ['final.evidence.episodes', 'result.top_program_01']\nThe fixed mean is .7*sin(XW)+.3*x0*cos(j), with W[l,j]=sin(l*(K-1)+j+1)/3. Shift changes only test X to clipped N(.8,1) in [-2.5,3], changing covariate support as well as frequency. sources= ['final.evidence.episodes', 'result.top_program_01']\nIndependent random streams use SeedSequence([seed,case,split,202611]); reference split index is 3. Frozen resources are checked for numeric row-content disjointness, positivity, closure and hashes. sources= ['final.evidence.episodes', 'result.top_program_01']\nPer-context mass resolution is 1/1024. Zero hits have a one-sided .95 upper bound 1-.05**(1/1024)=.00292124, not zero mass. Averaged plugin context standard errors do not quantify joint mass uncertainty. sources= ['final.evidence.episodes', 'result.top_program_01']\nThe evaluator resamples seed blocks 20000 times using RNG seed 62845. Paired utility intervals have optional tail probability .05/(2*54); volume intervals are unadjusted. Repeated uncertainty summaries are deduplicated. sources= ['final.evidence.episodes', 'result.top_program_01']\nConstant-input queries use training-mean features; nonidentity permutations rotate calibration and test rows separately. Whole-simplex and zero-radius references are degenerate metric controls, not useful calibrated baselines. sources= ['final.evidence.episodes', 'result.top_program_01']\nThe evaluated environment requires CPython 3.11 or 3.12 and NumPy 1.26.4 with native Linux isolation. No external dataset is downloaded for the synthetic study. sources= ['final.evidence.episodes', 'result.top_program_01']\n\nEXISTING ADAPTATION ABSTRACTS\nGuan2023 Summary \n                We propose a new inference framework called localized conformal prediction. It generalizes the framework of conformal prediction by offering a single-test-sample adaptive construction that emphasizes a local region around this test sample, and can be combined with different conformal scores. The proposed framework enjoys an assumption-free finite sample marginal coverage guarantee, and it also offers additional local coverage guarantees under suitable assumptions. We demonstrate how to change from conformal prediction to localized conformal prediction using several conformal scores, and we illustrate a potential gain via numerical examples.\nYang2024 Conformal prediction is a generic methodology for finite-sample valid distribution-free prediction. This technique has garnered a lot of attention in the literature partly because it can be applied with any machine learning algorithm that provides point predictions to yield valid prediction regions. Of course, the efficiency (width/volume) of the resulting prediction region depends on the performance of the machine learning algorithm. In the context of point prediction, several techniques (such as cross-validation) exist to select one of many machine learning algorithms for better performance. In contrast, such selection techniques are seldom discussed in the context of set prediction (or prediction regions). In this article, we consider the problem of obtaining the smallest conformal prediction region given a family of machine learning algorithms. We provide two general-purpose selection algorithms and consider coverage as well as width properties of the final prediction region. The first selection method yields the smallest width prediction region among the family of conformal prediction regions for all sample sizes but only has an approximate coverage guarantee. The second selection method has a finite sample coverage guarantee but only attains close to the smallest width. The approximate optimal width property of the second method is quantified via an oracle inequality. As an illustration, we consider the use of aggregation of nonparametric regression estimators in the split conformal method with the absolute residual conformal score. Supplementary materials for this article are available online, including a standardized description of the materials available for reproducing the work.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
